#!/bin/sh
# Debian post-install. dpkg has no interactive flow and runs as root, so nothing here
# may ask a question or write into a user's home. It prints the two commands that do
# the asking: julenny-toolkit-scripts (copies the scripts) and merge-claude-config.sh
# (adds the connector to Claude Desktop).
#
# Until v0.7.5 this text never mentioned the connector merge at all, so the script
# shipped in the package and was never run: the .deb installed an MCP server that no
# client was ever told about.
set -e

case "$1" in
    configure)
        cat <<'EOF'

JuLenny FHE Toolkit installed.

  julenny-toolkit --version    offline crypto CLI (no networking)
  julenny-mcp                  MCP server, for driving the flow from Claude

ONE WORKING FOLDER

Keys, datasets and results all live in a single folder, shared by the connector,
the scripts and the CLI. Put your own data files in it and all three can
see them. The default is ~/julenny-workdir. To use a different one, put its path
in ~/.config/julenny/workdir, or let the next command record it for you.

CONNECTOR (Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code)

To drive the flow from Claude Desktop, add the connector with your API key:

  /usr/share/julenny-toolkit/merge-claude-config.sh <api-key> \
      /usr/bin/julenny-mcp https://julenny.net JuLenny ~/julenny-workdir

Close Claude Desktop first: it rewrites its configuration when it exits and
would erase the entry. It needs jq. Run it as the user who will use Claude, not
as root, or the connector lands in root's configuration.

SCRIPTS

The scripts are the reference for integrating the toolkit into your own
pipelines. They ship read-only under /usr/share/julenny-toolkit/scripts. For a
copy you can run and edit:

  julenny-toolkit-scripts

It asks where to put the scripts, and places their sample data under samples/
in your JuLenny working folder, which is where the Claude connector reads it
too. It does NOT ask which side of a collaboration this machine is: there is
one set of scripts for both sides, and which side you are comes from the
permission you pick. Skip it if you only need the CLI or the MCP server.

EOF
        ;;
esac

exit 0
